a quick show of hands how many of you  remember playing with these as kids  awesome I for one can close my eyes and  reminisce the absolute sense of  imagination and creativity that these  blocks could evoke now imagine if you  could use these blocks to create a world  of your own a real world so that could  be something like you being sent back in  time to 16-24 to recreate the city of  New York how cool would that be can you  imagine the endless possibilities well  that's exactly the feeling that I got  when I looked at my job description  about six years back the project in this  instance was the first of its kind city  within a city concept in the world and  it was called the dubai international  financial centre so as it happened the  government of dubai had latched onto  this opportunity that there was no  financial center which was serving the  region between london and singapore now  I probably know what some of you are  thinking here you're probably thinking  well we know this region has a lot of  potential but then we also know that  this region is synonymous with certain  other words red tape corruption poor  infrastructure social and political  turmoil and you would be absolutely  correct however we are not today here to  talk about the present we are here to  talk about a future a future in which  you all could be global leaders so how  about we take a look at the macro  economic future for this part of the  world if we were having this  conversation around and say 2009 this  region's overall economic output would  have been something around forty percent  of the size of the US economy fast  forward on to 2020 this number could be  as high as fifty five percent and and by  2030 this number could be seventy  percent or even higher in a sense this  region would be imperative for your  business no matter what business you're  in and the cool part is that the trends  which are changing these numbers are  already underway about a decade back if  you were running an international  business your business would have been a  part of what was known as the east-west  trading corridor and the east-west  trading corridor used to contribute to  about eight point five dollars out of  every ten dollars exchanging hands  globally however in the last decade  there is another Force which is changing  the landscape of global trade completely  and this phenomena ladies and gentlemen  is known as a New Silk Route let's try  to understand this phenomenon through  the eyes of our first protagonist  Shanley now Shanley is a  first-generation Chinese entrepreneur  she started a small medical disposable  medical supplies business back in 2003  she was catering clients to  predominantly in Europe and in the  Americas however as we all know by 2009  things have changed the world was  different poor economic conditions and  the fact that regulations had become  really stringent in the West forced her  to consider other markets it was at that  point in time that she heard about this  whole story of growth in Africa and  India and the Middle East not  surprisingly 16 months later Shanley was  on a plane halfway across the globe and  she started a very small reacts port hub  in Dubai with just five employees in  2010 I was fortunate enough to meet  Shanley again and this time around boy  had the fortunes changed her five-man  operation had grown to over 100  employees and a business had grown  twelve hundred percent and let me assure  you Shalee is not an exception in fact  according to the World Bank this zone or  the south-south trading corridor  or the New Silk Route is going to  contribute to seven dollars out of every  ten dollars exchanging hands globally by  2030 I saw the proof of this growth  firsthand myself from the city that I  was privileged to start when we started  in 2004 these were the clients which  were registered with us 20 of them and  these are all of them I would love you  to guess as to how many clients that  this city hosted a 1400 plus and this is  just a small sample of the kind of  clients it hosts but then the business  and economics is just one part of the  equation there is a far greater social  force which is completely transforming  this part of the world now let me ask  you all a question at this point in time  if I asked all of you which country or  region in the world today produces the  highest proportion of women  entrepreneurs what will you answer be I  bet a lot of you would be saying Silicon  Valley but would you be surprised if I  said no it is not Silicon Valley but in  fact it's in places like Yemen and  Uganda we have forty percent of the  entrepreneurs of women let's try to  understand this phenomena to the eyes of  our second pet organist naja naja lies a  mother of four and she's a Saudi  national ever since she was 16 she  always wanted to start her own private  brand of code clothing label  unfortunately the society did not give  her much of a chance in fact apart from  a supportive husband nobody really came  to help and added to that the  regulations in Saudi Arabia for women  entrepreneurs was so stringent that she  was not even allowed to represent  herself in front of the government and  in spite of all these limitations nashla  finally broke through the glass ceiling  and started her own private label in  2012 and named it Sheila  now having seen modular and by the way  naturalizer one to your left now having  seen modular and having kind of heard a  story do you believe you really  understand the needs and aspirations of  Nigel as a customer and this is where  most multinationals make a mistake they  pick up a product or service from one  part of the world and take it to another  part without really understanding the  ground realities till you really  understand the trials and tribulations  of nodular do you really understand the  environment that she operates in it  won't really justify that Nigella gives  a business to you  so now going back to our example of the  Lego bricks this region is like a pack  of Lego bricks where all the pieces are  coming together some of these pieces are  broken some are not there and there are  others which are still under formation  and it would be your responsibility as  future leaders to put all these pieces  together and while you do that you need  to remember that there is no precedence  so you need to push the state of school  you need to be innovative but at the  same time you need to be patient and you  need to be respectful and there are a  couple of principles which I personally  like to follow which I would love to  share with you all first read but don't  always take the mass media too seriously  secondly learn but start by unlearning  and finally every opportunity that you  get go out and meet the nudge laws and  the Shanley's of the world try to soak  in a little bit of their culture and  their life and I must admit I made my  own fair share of mistakes and my first  stint in the Middle East I was supposed  to start a woman's only banking product  for one of the multinational clients and  I went into the market imagining them to  be my customers and I was not totally of  the mark they were in fact a part of the  segment that I was going to create it to  but I made one fundamental mistake I  forgot the other end of the spectrum so  let me try to end this presentation with  one of my favorite quotes that the mind  is like a parachute it does not work if  it doesn't open thank you  you  you
 

